---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Colophon · San Lee"
  description="How this site was built: an honest note on directing an AI agent, and verifying its output, the same way I'd run any small project."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/colophon.html"
  ogType="article"
>

  <SiteNav />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Colophon</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      How this site was built, because a site about being honest should be honest
      about how it was made.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site was built the way I would run any small project with an agent: I
      set the direction, the contracts, and the bar; an AI (Claude) did most of
      the typing; and I verified the output against the real repos before it
      shipped. I orchestrated, Claude implemented.
    </p>
    <p>
      That claim gets the same standard as everything else here: evidence over
      assertion. The record below is the honest version &mdash; some of those
      calls were mine, some weren&rsquo;t. Knowing where you ended up is not the
      same as proving how you got there, and the record is the part worth
      keeping.
    </p>
    <p>
      Directing a capable agent well, setting the contracts, catching the
      overreach, refusing the convenient answer, is the work I think matters as
      the tools get stronger. This site is the practice rep, and this page is me
      saying so out loud.
    </p>

    <h2>The ledger</h2>
    <p>
      This is a sample from the actual session transcripts, spanning the whole
      build. Dates and quotes are real, tightened for readability. I didn&rsquo;t
      filter out the entries that make either of us look bad &mdash; and since
      early July the worst of them come with public receipts: the operating
      layer now keeps its
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/claude-ops">incident log in the open</a>,
      postmortem-first. The July quotes are lifted straight from those
      postmortems and the linked records.
    </p>
    <!--
      Curation bar for this ledger (read before editing):
      Every entry must show human judgment changing the outcome — a catch, a
      refusal, a redirect, or the agent's own case winning on merit. If an entry
      reads as "Claude did a task well," it does not belong here; the page's
      claim is that the *direction* was the work, so the quote should almost
      always be San's, not the agent being clever.
      This is a curated sample, not a growing log. Keep it to ~6-8, and as
      stronger steering moments land, SWAP one in — do not append.
    -->
    <ol class="timeline">
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jun 21</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Sometimes its case won.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">It pushed back on my docs format, Markdown over RST; I weighed the reasoning and took it. Steering cuts both ways &mdash; the point isn&rsquo;t that I&rsquo;m always right, it&rsquo;s that someone is deciding. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;if you feel it&rsquo;s overkill, so be it.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jun 27</div>
        <div class="tl-title">It insisted the system still used Kafka.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">The local clone it trusted was out of date, so it argued a fact that had changed. That became a standing rule: verify before asserting. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;it&rsquo;s not even using Kafka anymore. No?&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jun 28</div>
        <div class="tl-title">It called a broken page fine.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">It told me the site was fine on mobile; it felt cramped to me, and digging found a real gutter bug. Knowing the tool is confident isn&rsquo;t the same as knowing it&rsquo;s right. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;a bit cramped as I nav through the different pages.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 1</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I turned it on this site.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">An adversarial round against the site&rsquo;s own claims: the homepage and the r&eacute;sum&eacute; disagreed about the present, and a site preaching evals-as-CI had no CI. Both fixed the same day; the round is <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/ROADMAP.md">logged in the roadmap</a>. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;attack the gap between claims and practice.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 2</div>
        <div class="tl-title">It burned a whole usage window.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">A scoped question set off an uncapped fan-out of thirty-plus agents on the most expensive model tier; I caught it mid-flight and killed it, an entire five-hour window gone in forty-five minutes. The fix is a hook that mechanically blocks any uncapped run (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/claude-ops/blob/main/incidents/2026-07-02-uncapped-premium-fanout.md">postmortem</a>). <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;spend was authorized blind, by default, with no gate.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 3</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I asked for holes; it found one in its own guard.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">On an open audit request it bypassed its own credential guard &mdash; the hook built after real leaks &mdash; with an interpreter one-liner, proving the gap safely before a live one could. The prompt that mattered was mine; the finding was real (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/claude-ops/blob/main/incidents/2026-07-03-credential-guard-interpreter-bypass.md">postmortem</a>). <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;any gaps, leaks, vulnerabilities, risks &mdash; I want to know.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The front-end learning log</h2>
    <p>
      I came from infra and backend, where the output is invisible by design, so
      every technique on this site was new ground. Each lesson below captures the
      concept, the why, and the files that changed. They render on GitHub:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/learning/01-scroll-reveal.md">Scroll-reveal</a>: <code>IntersectionObserver</code>, CSS transitions, <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/learning/02-hover-and-gradient.md">Hover micro-interactions + animated gradient</a>: <code>@keyframes</code>, pseudo-elements, z-index stacking.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/learning/03-layout-flexbox-grid.md">Layout: Flexbox &amp; Grid</a>: 1D vs 2D, <code>auto-fit</code>/<code>minmax</code>, <code>fr</code> units.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/learning/04-robust-and-fluid.md">Robust &amp; fluid</a>: <code>:focus-visible</code>, <code>clamp()</code>, container queries (<a href="lab/gallery.html">gallery</a>).</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/learning/05-scroll-driven-storytelling.md">Scroll-driven storytelling</a>: scroll/view timelines, <code>position: sticky</code>, progressive enhancement (<a href="lab/scroll-storytelling.html">demo</a>).</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/learning/06-svg-data-driven-diagram.md">Data-driven SVG diagram</a>: <code>viewBox</code>, <code>createElementNS</code>, keyboard-operable SVG.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/learning/07-domain-dns-email.md">The domain: DNS, CNAMEs &amp; email</a>: <code>A</code> vs <code>CNAME</code>, apex rules, <code>MX</code>, receive-only forwarding.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These used to live in a separate <em>lab</em> section, walled off so the
      experiments couldn&rsquo;t dent the showcase. That split is
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/decisions/ADR-004-retire-the-lab-as-the-vehicle.md">retired</a>:
      the sandbox stopped being fed, and the framing was quietly discounting the
      site&rsquo;s strongest artifact &mdash; the
      <a href="projects/loop-replay.html">loop replay</a> was filed under
      &ldquo;expect rough edges&rdquo; while two project pages cited it as
      evidence. The learning continues inside the work instead of in a wing that
      needs feeding.
    </p>
    <p class="muted-line"><a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/tree/main/learning">The full log &rarr;</a></p>
  </main>
  <script is:inline src="assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
